# LeakSanitizer suppressions for the HYBRID_SANITIZE build (issues #42, #47).
#
# CMake attaches this file (LSAN_OPTIONS) to every test when HYBRID_SANITIZE is
# ON, so a plain `ctest` in a sanitizer build reports exactly what CI does.
#
# Only the un-instrumented PQ dependencies are suppressed: liboqs and the PQ-only
# oqsprovider may keep one-time global init state that is released later than
# LSan's end-of-run check. There is deliberately NO `leak:libcrypto` (or
# `leak:libssl`) rule. All provider heap goes through CRYPTO_malloc, so such a
# rule would match, and mask, a leak in the provider's own code too.
#
# That works because every test is unload-clean: each one unloads the providers
# it loaded before freeing their libctx (test/prov_util.h), so provider teardown
# and the libctx's method-store cleanup run before LSan's check. OSSL_LIB_CTX_free
# alone does not drop an OSSL_PROVIDER_load reference; such a provider would be
# torn down only at OPENSSL_cleanup, after the check, and everything it holds
# would be reported. A new test must follow the same discipline.
#
# When this was set up (2026-11, OpenSSL 3.5 and 4.2-dev with liboqs 0.16), the
# whole in-process suite was leak-clean even with this file empty; the two rules
# are a margin for other liboqs/oqsprovider builds. If a genuine dependency init leak ever surfaces, suppress that specific
# function, never a whole library the provider allocates through.
leak:liboqs
leak:oqsprovider
